IMPORT prelude

-- Chemical Weapons (Prohibition) Act 2000 (Singapore) -- what the Act decides for a person or business that deals in chemicals:
-- "permitted purpose" and "chemical weapon" (s 2(1)), the chemical-weapons offences and their defences (s 8), when a licence from
-- the Director-General is needed (s 9(1) to (8)), the penalties for the Act's offences (ss 8, 9(12), (13), 12(2), 13(4), 14, 19(2),
-- 25, 26(3), 27(4)), obstruction of inspectors and the refusal-of-consent carve-out (s 25), confidentiality (s 26), extraterritorial
-- reach (s 5(1)) and the composition cap (s 30(1)).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/CWPA2000.txt. The deposit says the revised edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; the latest
-- amending Act annotated in the text is 2/2012 (s 26(2)). The deposit's metadata records retrieval on 1 October 2026 as the
-- "Current version as at 01 Oct 2026".
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0088 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. It asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper
-- question yet. SCOPED to the provisions a chemicals business or an individual actually meets. Not encoded: administration
-- (s 6), forfeiture (s 10), the reporting and record-keeping duties themselves (s 12(1), which turn on Parts VI to IX of the
-- Verification Annex and on regulations not deposited), the Director-General's notices (s 13(1) to (3)), inspection powers and
-- warrant procedure (ss 15 to 24, 27(1) to (3)), body-corporate liability (s 28), court jurisdiction (s 29), regulation-making
-- (ss 9(9), (10), (14), 32, 33) and the Schedule's chemical lists (a chemical's Schedule is taken as given). The "prescribed
-- concentration" for the mixture exemptions and the "prescribed" compoundable offences are in regulations that were not retrieved.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Chemical Weapons (Prohibition) Act 2000 -- offences and licences`

DECLARE Chemical IS ONE OF
    `a Schedule 1 chemical`
    `a Schedule 2 chemical`
    `a Schedule 3 chemical`
    `an unscheduled toxic chemical`

DECLARE Purpose IS ONE OF
    `research`
    `medical`
    `pharmaceutical`
    `protection against toxic chemicals or chemical weapons`
    `industrial`
    `agricultural`
    `another peaceful purpose`
    `a military purpose not using toxicity as a method of warfare`
    `law enforcement including domestic riot control`
    `causing harm through toxicity`

§§ `2(1) -- permitted purpose and chemical weapon`

-- s 2(1) "permitted purpose" means -- (a) "in the case of a Schedule 1 chemical, research, medical, pharmaceutical or protective
-- purpose"; (b) in the case of any other toxic chemical -- (i) "industrial, agricultural, research, medical, pharmaceutical or any
-- other peaceful purpose"; (ii) protection against toxic chemicals and chemical weapons; (iii) "any military purpose not connected
-- with the use of chemical weapons and not dependent on the use of the toxic properties of chemicals as a method of warfare"; or
-- (iv) "any law enforcement purpose (including domestic riot control)".
-- (Inference: "protective purpose" in (a) is read as the protection purpose in (b)(ii).)
GIVEN `the chemical` IS A Chemical, `the purpose` IS A Purpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the purpose is permitted` IF
    CONSIDER `the chemical`
    WHEN `a Schedule 1 chemical` THEN
        CONSIDER `the purpose`
        WHEN `research` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `medical` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `pharmaceutical` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `protection against toxic chemicals or chemical weapons` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `the purpose`
        WHEN `causing harm through toxicity` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 2(1) "chemical weapon" (a): "a toxic chemical and its precursors, except where intended for a permitted purpose, as long as
-- the type and quantity are consistent with that purpose". (Munitions and equipment, limbs (b) and (c), are not encoded.)
GIVEN `the chemical` IS A Chemical, `the purpose` IS A Purpose, `type and quantity consistent with the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the chemical is a chemical weapon` IF
    NOT (`the purpose is permitted` `the chemical` `the purpose` AND `type and quantity consistent with the purpose`)

§§ `5(1) -- reach outside Singapore`

-- s 5(1): "Sections 8 and 26 extend to acts done or omitted to be done outside Singapore by -- (a) any citizen of Singapore; or
-- (b) any other person on board any ship or aircraft registered in Singapore." (3): proceedings for such an offence need the
-- consent of the Public Prosecutor. (Inference: the Act's other offences, which s 5 does not extend, reach only acts in Singapore.)
DECLARE Provision IS ONE OF
    `section 8`
    `section 26`
    `another offence provision`

GIVEN `the provision` IS A Provision, `done in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN, `by a citizen` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `on a Singapore-registered ship or aircraft` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the provision reaches the act` IF
        `done in Singapore`
    OR  (    CONSIDER `the provision`
             WHEN `another offence provision` THEN FALSE
             OTHERWISE TRUE
         AND (`by a citizen` OR `on a Singapore-registered ship or aircraft`))

§§ `8 -- use, etc., of chemical weapons`

-- s 8(1): any person who (a) uses, (b) develops or produces, (c) acquires, stockpiles or retains, (d) transfers, directly or
-- indirectly, a chemical weapon; (e) engages in military preparations to use one; (f) "knowingly assists, encourages or induces"
-- another to engage in an activity prohibited to a State Party; or (g) "uses a riot control agent as a method of warfare".
-- (2): nothing in (1) applies to an act done by an authorised officer, a national inspector or an international inspector "under
-- the authority of this Act or any other written law".
-- (3): for (1)(a), (c), (d), (e) or (g) "it is a defence for the accused to prove" that (a) the accused "neither knew nor suspected
-- nor had reason to suspect" the object was a chemical weapon or riot control agent; or (b) "as soon as reasonably practicable"
-- after first knowing or suspecting, took all reasonable steps to inform an authorised officer. The defence is NOT given for (b)
-- developing or producing, or (f) assisting.
DECLARE Conduct IS ONE OF
    `using`
    `developing or producing`
    `acquiring, stockpiling or retaining`
    `transferring`
    `military preparations to use`
    `knowingly assisting a prohibited activity`
    `using a riot control agent as a method of warfare`

GIVEN `the conduct` IS A Conduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 8(3) defence is open` IF
    CONSIDER `the conduct`
    WHEN `developing or producing` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `knowingly assisting a prohibited activity` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the conduct` IS A Conduct, `the elements are made out` IS A BOOLEAN, `an officer or inspector acting under authority` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved no knowledge or reason to suspect` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved prompt report to an authorised officer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `guilty under s 8` IF
        `the elements are made out`
    AND (NOT `an officer or inspector acting under authority`)
    AND NOT (    `the s 8(3) defence is open` `the conduct`
             AND (`proved no knowledge or reason to suspect` OR `proved prompt report to an authorised officer`))

-- s 8(1)(h), (i): "imprisonment for a term which may extend to life imprisonment; and ... a fine not exceeding $1 million". The
-- two limbs are joined by "and", where every other offence in the Act says "or to both". (Inference: both are imposed.)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 8 maximum fine` MEANS 1000000

§§ `9(1) to (8) -- when a licence is needed`

-- s 9(1): a person must not "use, develop, produce, acquire, stockpile, retain or transfer a Schedule 1 chemical for a permitted
-- purpose" except under a licence granted by the Director-General. (5): (1) does not apply to a mixture "containing not more than the
-- prescribed concentration of a prescribed Schedule 1 chemical".
-- (2): no licence-free (a) producing, processing or consuming a Schedule 2 chemical, or (b) producing a Schedule 3 chemical, for a
-- permitted purpose. (6): (2) does not apply to the like mixtures.
-- (4): a person must not "import or export a Schedule 2 chemical or Schedule 3 chemical" except under a licence. (8): (4) does not
-- apply to (a) "the import of a mixture" within the prescribed concentration, or (b) "the export to a country that is a party to the
-- Convention" of such a mixture.
-- "transfer" includes import and export (s 2(1)). Processing and consuming are not words of s 9(1). (Inference: processing or
-- consuming a Schedule 1 chemical is a "use" of it.) Holding, using or acquiring a Schedule 2 or 3 chemical needs no s 9 licence.
DECLARE Dealing IS ONE OF
    `use`
    `develop`
    `produce`
    `process`
    `consume`
    `acquire`
    `stockpile`
    `retain`
    `import`
    `export`
    `transfer within Singapore`

GIVEN `the chemical` IS A Chemical, `the dealing` IS A Dealing, `a mixture within the prescribed concentration` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `exported to a Convention party` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a licence is needed` IF
    CONSIDER `the chemical`
    WHEN `a Schedule 1 chemical` THEN NOT `a mixture within the prescribed concentration`
    WHEN `a Schedule 2 chemical` THEN
        CONSIDER `the dealing`
        WHEN `produce` THEN NOT `a mixture within the prescribed concentration`
        WHEN `process` THEN NOT `a mixture within the prescribed concentration`
        WHEN `consume` THEN NOT `a mixture within the prescribed concentration`
        WHEN `import` THEN NOT `a mixture within the prescribed concentration`
        WHEN `export` THEN NOT (`a mixture within the prescribed concentration` AND `exported to a Convention party`)
        OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHEN `a Schedule 3 chemical` THEN
        CONSIDER `the dealing`
        WHEN `produce` THEN NOT `a mixture within the prescribed concentration`
        WHEN `import` THEN NOT `a mixture within the prescribed concentration`
        WHEN `export` THEN NOT (`a mixture within the prescribed concentration` AND `exported to a Convention party`)
        OTHERWISE FALSE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 9(3): a person must not produce "(a) more than 200 tonnes in total of any one or more unscheduled discrete organic chemicals per
-- year; or (b) more than 30 tonnes of any one unscheduled discrete organic chemical containing phosphorous, sulfur or fluorine per
-- year" except under a licence. (7): mixtures within the prescribed concentration are disregarded in the count (the inputs are the
-- tonnages after that is done). (15): "unscheduled discrete organic chemical" means one "not listed in the Schedule".
GIVEN `total tonnes of unscheduled discrete organic chemicals a year` IS A NUMBER,
      `largest tonnes of one such chemical containing phosphorous, sulfur or fluorine a year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a production licence is needed for discrete organic chemicals` IF
        `total tonnes of unscheduled discrete organic chemicals a year` GREATER THAN 200
    OR  `largest tonnes of one such chemical containing phosphorous, sulfur or fluorine a year` GREATER THAN 30

§§ `9(12), (13), 12(2), 13(4), 14, 19(2), 25(1), 26(3), 27(4) -- maximum penalties`

-- s 9(12): Schedule 1 without a licence -- "$100,000 or ... 10 years or ... both". s 9(13): (2), (3) or (4) -- "$10,000 or ...
-- 2 years". s 12(2): reporting and records -- "$6,000 or ... 6 months". s 13(4): Director-General's notice -- "$6,000 or ...
-- 6 months". s 14: false or misleading document -- "$10,000 or ... 2 years". s 19(2): wilfully failing a written direction --
-- "$6,000 or ... 12 months". s 25(1): obstructing an inspector -- "$15,000 or ... 12 months". s 26(3): confidentiality -- "$6,000
-- or ... 12 months". s 27(4): obstructing a search -- "$15,000 or ... 12 months". Each may be both.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `Schedule 1 dealing without a licence`
    `other dealing without a licence`
    `failing to report or keep records`
    `failing to comply with a notice`
    `false or misleading document`
    `failing to comply with a written direction`
    `obstructing an inspector`
    `breaching confidentiality`
    `obstructing a search`

GIVEN `the offence` IS A Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `Schedule 1 dealing without a licence` THEN 100000
    WHEN `other dealing without a licence` THEN 10000
    WHEN `failing to report or keep records` THEN 6000
    WHEN `failing to comply with a notice` THEN 6000
    WHEN `false or misleading document` THEN 10000
    WHEN `failing to comply with a written direction` THEN 6000
    WHEN `obstructing an inspector` THEN 15000
    WHEN `breaching confidentiality` THEN 6000
    WHEN `obstructing a search` THEN 15000

GIVEN `the offence` IS A Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `Schedule 1 dealing without a licence` THEN 120
    WHEN `other dealing without a licence` THEN 24
    WHEN `failing to report or keep records` THEN 6
    WHEN `failing to comply with a notice` THEN 6
    WHEN `false or misleading document` THEN 24
    WHEN `failing to comply with a written direction` THEN 12
    WHEN `obstructing an inspector` THEN 12
    WHEN `breaching confidentiality` THEN 12
    WHEN `obstructing a search` THEN 12

§§ `25 -- obstructing an inspector, and refusing consent`

-- s 25(1): "Any person who wilfully obstructs, hinders, resists, or deceives any national inspector or international inspector".
-- (2), (3): "Nothing in this section applies to a refusal to give consent to entry" by an inspector "who is not acting pursuant to
-- a warrant" (s 21 or 22 for a national inspector; s 22 for an international inspector).
GIVEN `wilfully obstructed, hindered, resisted or deceived` IS A BOOLEAN, `the only obstruction was refusing consent to entry` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the inspector had a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `guilty of obstructing an inspector` IF
        `wilfully obstructed, hindered, resisted or deceived`
    AND ((NOT `the only obstruction was refusing consent to entry`) OR `the inspector had a warrant`)

§§ `26 -- confidentiality`

-- s 26(1): "Every person must keep confidential any information that is given pursuant to this Act or the Convention concerning
-- the affairs of another person." (2): it may be disclosed only with the consent of that person "or for the purpose of -- (a)
-- enabling Singapore to fulfil its obligations under the Convention; (b) the enforcement of this Act; or (c) dealing with an
-- emergency involving public safety or ensuring the security of Singapore". (3): failing to comply is an offence.
DECLARE DisclosurePurpose IS ONE OF
    `Singapore's obligations under the Convention`
    `enforcement of this Act`
    `a public-safety emergency or Singapore's security`
    `a commercial purpose`
    `reporting to the press`

GIVEN `the purpose of disclosure` IS A DisclosurePurpose, `the person concerned consented` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the disclosure is allowed` IF
        `the person concerned consented`
    OR  CONSIDER `the purpose of disclosure`
        WHEN `a commercial purpose` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `reporting to the press` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `30(1) -- composition`

-- s 30(1): the Director-General may compound an offence "prescribed as a compoundable offence" by collecting a sum not exceeding
-- "(a) $1,000 in respect of any offence where the maximum fine prescribed for that offence is less than $5,000; and (b) $5,000 in
-- respect of any other offence". Every offence encoded above has a maximum fine of $6,000 or more, so within the Act itself (b)
-- always governs; (a) can bite only for offences created by regulations (s 33(2)(e) allows fines up to $10,000). (Inference.)
GIVEN `maximum fine for the offence` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most that may be collected to compound` MEANS
    IF `maximum fine for the offence` LESS THAN 5000 THEN 1000 ELSE 5000
